A||N||15||Seanh||What should I do on my 3.4L swap?||||3||
Z||000000||Seanh||11-16-2000||10:13 PM||||I have been talking to some people about things to do to the 3.4 Pushrod to get the most power from it. I am wanting some more information on things to do. What I am doing so far is:<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Heads w/Stainless steel racing valves, 5 angle valve job, backcut, pocket ported, positive valve seals and new valve guides.<BR><LI>TB bored to 57mm and matching Plenum<BR><LI>Plenum with boost box installed<BR><LI>Port matched middle intake manifold<BR><LI>Lower intake ported thru injector holes to restore an even spray pattern<BR><LI>GM 12364059 Cam w/lifters (If anyone had any better ideas let me know)<BR><LI>New pushrods<BR><LI>Accel or MD6 coil<BR><LI>8.8 Accel race wires<BR></UL><BR>Anyone have any other ideas of things I can do to this engine?<BR>Thanks<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"If you smoke after sex, you're doing it way to fast".</I>||||reg||3
Z||000001||mshill||11-16-2000||11:46 PM||||Sean,  I am looking at some of the same stuff.  Since I am not taking the heads off the long block I am skipping the head work.  What's a "boost box" for the plenum?  Instead of the cam I am looking at 1.6:1 roller tip rockers (don't want to pull the cam either).<P>You might also consider an underdrive pulley (power gains are debatable).  I am also looking at the $300 e-Ram electric blower (at 1.5 psi it isn't really a "super"charger).<P>What kind of exhaust are you looking at?  For now I was going to just weld and port my stock ones until I can afford and justify a set of headers.  I was also considering no muffler and some resonator tips but it might be too loud.  I will have to keep my cat for smog here.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||11-17-2000||12:13 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Plenum_with_top_1.jpg"> <BR>This is the idea for a booster box. It will help with two things. First, it will increase the volum of air available when I floor it, and it will allow access to polish rhe manifold.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FieroGTRacingInc||11-17-2000||01:04 AM||||seanh...  did you make that nox add on there??||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||11-17-2000||01:24 AM||||TWIN TURBOS!!!!<P>hehehehehe... Just yankin yer chain, pal. I would like to hear more theory on the "booster box".  Wouldn't that open space (I'm assuming the top of the plenum is milled out under the box) actually cause turbulence in the plenum under low vacuum driving environment? And wouldn't the volume of this chamber add only a negligible amount of "boost" and only for the minimal amount of time?  It seems to me this would add very little benefit and would actually decrease drivability.<P>Now, by no means am I a physicist, nor schooled in the area of fluid dynamics.  Shoot, I couldn't even tell you what size speaker box to use to mount 2 12" woofers in the console. I'm just playing devil's advocate and trying to provide food for thought.<P>Sure does look pretty butch though.<P>(Post 1100! Yippee)<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||11-17-2000||07:33 AM||||Tis' my off the top of my head idea, but Darrell Morse put the technical design into a physical form. Email him if you would like to know some more about. I was thinking the same thing about creating turbulance. And yes, it wouldnt provide much air, but it would help wih throttle responce some. He hasnt dynoed it yet, but I am hoping I can get it dynoed and see what kind of increase (or decrease) there auctually is. Twin Turbos, I do still have them, from an Eagle Talon. Maybe the could work on this engine, who knows.||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||11-17-2000||08:24 AM||||Don't forget to put the "Fiero" decal on top of the booster box.  Otherwise it'll never work right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Not to insult your intelligence or anything, but please be sure to check the clearance between the top of the plenum and the hood.  My memory tells me there isn't a lot of extra height available - unless you decide to put a rear hood scoop on, etc.  A lump of clay on top of the existing plenum will give you some data to work with (also, gives us grownups a good excuse to mess with Play-Dough!).  Leave enough room for engine deflection, and you're there.   ||||reg||4
Z||000007||Shaun41178||11-17-2000||02:51 PM||||mshill:  were you going to weld and port your stock exhaust manifolds or were you going to have someone else do it?  Just to let you know I already have a set available that have been welded and ported out.  You can have them for $120 plus your old manifolds in exchange.  If you keep your old ones the price will be $170.  If anyone wants this done I can do it for you too.  <P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||11-17-2000||05:31 PM||||Checked the clearance. On the deck lids with the "hump" in the middle (like my 86GT) it will work with, but the flat ones it will not. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||11-17-2000||06:40 PM||||I have some ideas that may help you out. I have dyno2000 and dragstrip2000 and I have done a lot of dynos for people here on the forum to help them get the most for there money and give them an idea of what to expect for performance from there modifications.<P>I think that porting the heads is a good modification along with the valve job and SI stainless steel valves. I am doing these same things to my 3.4-engine. I think the ported throttle body is not necessary and only will give you about 1-3 horsepower max according to dyno2000 across the rpm band. If you want an even spray pattern on your injectors you should send them to be blueprinted, flow checked and balanced. You can send them to <A HREF="http://www.injector.com." TARGET=_blank>www.injector.com.</A>  The injector cleaning service is good for a few horsepower/ torque and a better running engine. I think Stimpy is right about that plenum with the “boost box”. Your likely to cause turbulence in the plenum, which will cause a loss of air flow. The fiero induction system is a good free flowing system and I don’t think that this box is a good idea. The GM 12364059 camshaft is a great cam for this motor if you like a motor that makes its power down low. The power drops off sharply after 5000 rpm but that camshaft makes lots of low-end torque, which is good for automatic transmissions. If you want more of a drag racing camshaft than I recommend the GM 12363220 camshaft with the manual transmission only. The camshaft you have now works better with the automatic transmission. <P>With the above modifications and ported exhaust manifolds your engine will make about 173 horsepower @ 4500-5000 rpm and 221-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000-3500 rpm. With the five speed manual transmission your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.13 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.4 seconds @ 92.5 mph if you launch @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm. With the automatic transmission (3.18 final gear ratio) your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.92 seconds and the quarter mile in 15.19 seconds @ 89.9 mph.<P>With that same engine and the GM 12363220 camshaft your engine will make 180 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 216-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. With the manual transmission your fiero will go 0-60 in 5.96 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.25 seconds @ 93.9 mph if you launch again @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm. With the automatic transmission your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.99 seconds and the quarter mile in 15.16 seconds @ 90.7 mph. <P>Some modifications I would consider if I were you instead of the ported throttle body and “boost box” are FOCOA headers, a performance exhaust system like the Borla exhaust (www.fieroparts.com), a custom prom chip, and an aluminum flywheel (http://www.fieroshop.net). <P>Just by adding headers to your engine your power goes up from 173 HP @ 4500-5000 RPM to 197 HP @ 5000 rpm and 236 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. With the GM 12363220 camshaft it goes to 203 HP @ 5500 rpm and 231 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm. With the headers and the GM 12363220 camshaft your fiero will go 0-60 in 5.49 seconds and the quarter mile in 13.82 seconds @ 96.8 mph if you launch @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm. With the automatic transmission and the GM 12364059 camshaft your fiero will go 0-60 in 6.3 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.62 seconds @ 93.6 mph. <P>I just want to say that this is just a simulation and although I have found it to be very accurate when I compare it to actual results this does not mean that your fiero will get these exact numbers. They will just be very close. These numbers are also with a perfect running engine as far as air fuel ratios are concerned and that is why I recommend you have a custom prom chip made, possibly with you car on a real dynamometer to get the best possible results.<P>Good luck and I hope I helped.    <P> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||11-17-2000||09:28 PM||||Wow, that is some great information. Thanks alot. Also, before I start with my performance questions, where can I get that dyno2K program? From seeing this information, heres what I have decided.<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Changing to GM 12363220 Camshaft (Would you reccomend I do this, even though I have an Auto. I like the performance numbers for it a little more then the one I was going to go with before. I dont think 5 Ft-lbs of Torque loss is going to hurt me much.)<BR><LI>Going with FOCOA headers (I hate to put out that kind of money, but it really provides some power)<BR><LI>I was already going to use a Borla cat-back exhaust system along with high-flow cat.<BR><LI>New PROM chip<BR><LI>Aluminum Flywheel (already planning on doing this, but forgot to mention it above)<BR><LI>Not doing the Airbox idea<BR><LI>Still doing the TB and intake manifold porting and matching.<BR><LI>I already had my injectors cleaned at Injector.com about 2 weeks ago. I didnt notice much more then a smoother idle, and quieter injectors.<BR></UL><BR>Could you run the car on dyno again with the above modifications? It is an auto, with Fiero store shift kit.<P>EDIT: How much are the FOCOA headers you reccomend? If I decide to go with them in the end, would some one be willing to get them for me since I am not a member?<P>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 11-17-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 11-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Shaun41178||11-17-2000||10:57 PM||||I do think that you should go with the ported TB.  I picked up 2-3 hp throughout the rpm band and 4 peak throughout the entire rpm band.  A dyno sheet was posted here about a week ago of my results.  Torque improved the same amount.  This was with just a internally stock 2.8 as well and did not include the matched upper plenum.  Increases on the 3.4 should be greater as the 3.4 requires more airflow.  If it doesn't give more then 2-4 hp you will at least get a minimum of 2-4.<P>The boost box is up to you.  dyno test it and see if it gets more power then a stock one.<P>I do not recommend getting the GM 12363220 camshaft.  I was going to get this one as well but it does not include lifters.  Buying lifters from GM is expensive.  I just recommend getting the first cam you were planning on getting as it includes lifters and will save you quite a bit of money.<P>I do suggest an underdrive pulley as well.  I picked up roughly .2 in the qtr mile with this mod.<P>I am skeptical of the 25 hp gain from the Focoa headers according to the desktop dyno.  Headers are much more complicated then just typing in headers on a computer program.  for one length plays an important roll in how much power they will make along with collector size and tube diameter.  These might give more peak torque but could lose low end torque which if I am not mistaken is what you didn't want.<P>If you are going to go with headers I would suggest one from Mike Lecompte(hyptnotise) to get the most hp from.<P>I do not suggest getting a chip unless you have one custom made for your engine and spend time on the dyno making sure you get it done right.  doing that could cost a bit of money just in dyno time let alone chip programming<P>like 88 formula said its not an exact program but it kind of gives you a semi accurate idea of what kind of power your engine will make.  Only way to trully know is to hit a dyno.  It is hard for the program to give an accurate reading of hp and torque without flow numbers of the heads.  Its a good ballpark though.<P><P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>2.8 With nitrous<BR>Soon to have a 3.2 with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds<BR><A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Performance</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||11-18-2000||02:37 AM||||I want to emphasize that this program is very accurate when you use it correctly.  I have the fiero head flow numbers ported and unported. I got them from Norris racing <A HREF="http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html</A>   and I used the ported head flow numbers in the above dynos. I know that not all head flow numbers are the same but they are a lot closer than just guessing. With this program you don’t just type in headers and get a universal dyno. There are menus with seven different categories each representing the typical characteristic of that type of manifold or header, which take into consideration the collector size and tube diameter. 25 horsepower is what FOCOA claims there headers give to a 2.8-liter engine although I have never seen actual dyno sheets from them to back up this claim. The difference was 24 horsepower. <P>I will use an example that we all can relate to. My 88 formula is rated at 135 horsepower @ 4500 rpm and 165 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3600 rpm from GM. In the brooklands book the last article is form Road and Track where they compare a white 88 formula with a supercharged Toyota MR2. In that article the 88 formula went 0-60 mph in 8 seconds and the quarter mile in 16 seconds @ 85.5 mph according to Road and Track with the five speed manual transmission. <P>Dyno2000 came up with 134 horsepower @ 4500 rpm and 180 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. Using this dyno in Dragstrip2000 it came up with 0-60 in 7.8 seconds and the quarter mile in 15.6 seconds @ 86 mph if you launch @ 3500 rpm and shift @ 6000 rpm. These numbers are very close. There are a lot of factors that effect drag racing performance like track conditions, weather, and temperature. You can change all these perimeters with Dragstrip2000.  For example I used the following perimeters in the above simulations, humidity 60%, barometer 29.05, wind 0 mph, elevation 800 ft., and temperature 75 degrees. If I change humidity to 85%, barometer to 27.80, wind to 5 mph, and elevation to 2000 ft. that same formula goes 0-60 in 8.5 seconds and the quarter mile in 16.1 seconds @ 83.8 mph.<P>Seanh I would use the GM 12364059 camshaft if you want a fast street car. It gives better of the line acceleration than the GM 12363220 camshaft does in an automatic fiero because of the greater low-end torque. <P>With the GM 12364059 camshaft I got 0-60 mph in 6.27 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.47 seconds @ 96.8 mph. <P>With the GM 12363220 camshaft I got 0-60 mph in 6.34 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.47 seconds @ 97.1 mph.<P>With the stock 3.4 camshaft I got 0-60 mph in 6.5 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.62 seconds @ 96 mph.<P>You can get the FOCOA headers directly from FOCOA without being a member by just calling them or you can get them from most of these places <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/stores.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/stores.htm</A>  They cost around $600, which I know is steep. I can’t tell you about the header’s hypontinose mentioned because I know nothing about them. I have seen the FOCOA headers on many fiero’s and I can tell you that those headers are very well made high quality headers. The owners I talked to with the headers all said they were very happy, they fit very well, and they noticed a difference in performance. One guy I talked to said to me with the FOCOA headers on his 2.8-liter engine “ it definitely has more nut now”.   <BR> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Seanh||11-18-2000||04:39 AM||||Thanks. I think then, from hearing what you guys said, to stick with the original cam I was useing. I know how Dyno2K works, we have a demo version at the shop, but I would like the full version. Well, Today I am going to put together all the prices of everthing I need, so that cant be pretty. Thanks again for all the info. Where can I purchase the Dyno2K program anyways? And how much is it?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||11-18-2000||04:50 AM||||Also, are the FOCOA headers the best one to get for my application? I was looking at the Chris West ones a while back, but his website doesnt say much about the pipe sizes or anything. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||11-18-2000||04:04 PM||||You can get these programs here <A HREF="http://www.motionsoftware.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.motionsoftware.com</A>  <P>I don't know much about the Christ West headers but just by looking at the pictures on there web-site they don't look as nice as the FOCOA headers and the price is about the same. There was a guy here who had those headers and had a problem with them. He claimed they leaked at one of the welds after he had them Jet Hot coated.<BR>||||reg||1
